This version keeps the actual useful bills in two stacks. The stack order does not matter, but it makes taking a bill for change very direct. A $20 payment first removes one $10 and one $5 when possible, then falls back to three $5 bills.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    public boolean lemonadeChange(int[] bills) {
        Deque<Integer> fives = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<Integer> tens = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int bill : bills) {
            if (bill == 5) {
                fives.push(5);
            } else if (bill == 10) {
                if (fives.isEmpty()) {
                    return false;
                }
                fives.pop();
                tens.push(10);
            } else if (bill == 20) {
                if (!tens.isEmpty() && !fives.isEmpty()) {
                    tens.pop();
                    fives.pop();
                } else if (fives.size() >= 3) {
                    fives.pop();
                    fives.pop();
                    fives.pop();
                } else {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

For `[5, 10, 20]`, the $5 is used for the $10 customer. The next customer needs $10 + $5, but neither is available, so the method stops with `false`.

This does not modify the input array. Since the stacks can grow with the number of $5 bills, the time complexity is `O(n)` and the extra space is `O(n)`.

The cash drawer only needs two counts. A $10 customer needs one $5 bill, while a $20 customer needs either $10 + $5 or three $5 bills. For $20, I use the $10 + $5 option first so more $5 bills stay available.

```cpp
class Solution{
  public:
    bool lemonadeChange(vector<int>& bills){
        int five = 0;
        int ten = 0;

        for (int bill : bills) {
            if (bill == 5) {
                ++five;
            } else if (bill == 10) {
                if (five == 0) {
                    return false;
                }
                --five;
                ++ten;
            } else {
                if (ten > 0 && five > 0) {
                    --ten;
                    --five;
                } else if (five >= 3) {
                    five -= 3;
                } else {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[5, 5, 5, 10, 20]`: the first three bills give three $5 bills. The $10 uses one $5, leaving two $5 bills and one $10. The $20 uses those two denominations, so the result is `true`.

The input vector is only read, not changed. Time is `O(n)` and extra space is `O(1).`

There are only three possible bill values, so a small frequency array is enough. Index `0` stores the number of $5 bills, index `1` stores $10 bills, and index `2` stores $20 bills. The last count is not needed for change, but storing it keeps the denomination table clear.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public bool LemonadeChange(List<int> bills)
    {
        int[] count = new int[3];

        foreach (int bill in bills)
        {
            int index = bill / 10;
            count[index]++;

            if (bill == 5)
            {
                continue;
            }

            if (bill == 10)
            {
                if (count[0] == 0)
                {
                    return false;
                }
                count[0]--;
            }
            else
            {
                if (count[0] > 0 && count[1] > 0)
                {
                    count[0]--;
                    count[1]--;
                }
                else if (count[0] >= 3)
                {
                    count[0] -= 3;
                }
                else
                {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

The bill is added to the array before change is removed. That is safe because a $20 bill is not considered for change, and a received $10 bill cannot be used to pay its own $5 change.

For `[20]`, the array records the $20, then the code finds no valid $15 combination and returns `false`. The list is only read. Time is `O(n)` and extra space is `O(1)`.

A small map keeps the bill counts without storing every note separately. The received $20 bills do not need to be saved because they can never be used as change. When a $20 arrives, the code tries a $10 and a $5 before using three $5 bills.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public boolean lemonadeChange(int[] bills) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();
        count.put(5, 0);
        count.put(10, 0);

        for (int bill : bills) {
            if (bill == 5) {
                count.put(5, count.get(5) + 1);
            } else if (bill == 10) {
                if (count.get(5) == 0) {
                    return false;
                }
                count.put(5, count.get(5) - 1);
                count.put(10, count.get(10) + 1);
            } else if (bill == 20) {
                if (count.get(10) > 0 && count.get(5) > 0) {
                    count.put(10, count.get(10) - 1);
                    count.put(5, count.get(5) - 1);
                } else if (count.get(5) >= 3) {
                    count.put(5, count.get(5) - 3);
                } else {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

An empty input returns `true`, since there are no customers to reject. A first bill of $10 or $20 returns `false` because the drawer starts empty.

Each map operation here uses one of only two keys, so the run takes `O(n)` time and `O(1)` extra space.

Instead of storing counts, this keeps the usable bills in a list. For a $10 bill, it removes one $5. For a $20 bill, it searches for the preferred $10 and $5 pair, or removes three $5 bills. This is easier to see as a direct cash simulation, although it uses more time than counters.

```javascript
class Solution {
    lemonadeChange(bills) {
        const drawer = [];

        for (const bill of bills) {
            if (bill === 5) {
                drawer.push(5);
            } else if (bill === 10) {
                const fiveIndex = drawer.indexOf(5);
                if (fiveIndex === -1) {
                    return false;
                }
                drawer.splice(fiveIndex, 1);
                drawer.push(10);
            } else if (bill === 20) {
                const tenIndex = drawer.indexOf(10);
                const fiveIndex = drawer.indexOf(5);

                if (tenIndex !== -1 && fiveIndex !== -1) {
                    drawer.splice(Math.max(tenIndex, fiveIndex), 1);
                    drawer.splice(Math.min(tenIndex, fiveIndex), 1);
                } else {
                    let removed = 0;
                    while (removed < 3) {
                        const currentFive = drawer.indexOf(5);
                        if (currentFive === -1) {
                            return false;
                        }
                        drawer.splice(currentFive, 1);
                        ++removed;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

The `$20` branch checks the larger note first. That matters because spending three $5 bills can leave no $5 for a later $10 customer.

The input array is not changed, but the drawer list is built during the scan. Searching and removing can take `O(n)` for one customer, so the worst-case time is `O(n^2)` and extra space is `O(n)`.

